<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Alexandre de Calonne</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1734">1734</a>–<a href="/years/1802">1802</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles Alexandre de Calonne was a French statesman who served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a>'s controller-general of finances from 1783 to 1787. Faced with a growing deficit, he proposed a general land tax paid by all landowners, including the privileged, and summoned an Assembly of Notables in 1787 to approve it. The notables refused, he was dismissed, and the crisis led to the calling of the Estates-General in 1789.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-alexandre-de-calonne.jpg" alt="Charles Alexandre de Calonne" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Élisabeth Louise Vigée Le Brun · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vig%C3%A9e-Lebrun,_Elisabeth-Louise_-_Charles-Alexandre_de_Calonne_(1734-1802)_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles Alexandre de Calonne was born on 20 January 1734 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douai">Douai</a> into an upper-class family. He entered the legal profession and became a lawyer to the general council of Artois, procureur to the parlement of Douai and Master of Requests, then intendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> in 1768 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a> in 1774. He seems to have had notable business abilities and an entrepreneurial spirit. He owed his appointment as Controller-General of Finances, which he took up on 3 November 1783, to the Comte de Vergennes, who supported him for over three years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On taking office Calonne found that the nation had debts of 110 million livres, partly incurred through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s involvement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>. At first he tried to maintain confidence through loans and through building projects and spending, reissued gold coinage in October 1785 and developed the Caisse d'Escompte. He presented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> with a larger plan on 20 August 1786. Its heart was a new land value tax to replace the old vingtième taxes and sweep away the fiscal exemptions of the privileged orders, to be run through provincial assemblies elected by property owners. It also proposed free trade in grain and the abolition of internal customs barriers.</p><p>Knowing that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parlement-of-paris">Parlement of Paris</a> would reject such a tax, Calonne persuaded the king to call a hand-picked Assembly of Notables, which met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> in February 1787. The notables balked at the size of the deficit and suspected the controller-general of being responsible for it. Protests against him followed, and effigies of him were burned. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> dismissed him on 8 April 1787 and exiled him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> he was known as Monsieur Déficit. Loménie de Brienne replaced him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Calonne went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>. In 1789 he crossed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> hoping to stand for election to the Estates-General but was forbidden to enter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and he joined the émigrés at Coblenz. He was with the Count of Artois at Pillnitz in August 1791. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> allowed him to return to France in 1802, and he died about a month after his arrival, on 30 October 1802.</p><p>Historians judge him differently. Munro Price considers his reputation for the financial crisis unfair, while Eugene White stresses that he continued a venal system of financial administration. His fall revealed the monarchy's financial instability and the lack of public scrutiny of its accounts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles Alexandre de Calonne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1787 the finance minister <b>Calonne</b> put a reform plan to an Assembly of Notables, which rejected it, and Louis XVI agreed to summon the Estates-General, which met at Versailles in May 1789.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douai">Douai</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.37,3.08&amp;year=1778" aria-label="Douai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4620 413 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Charles Alexandre de Calonne"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5070 683h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5070 683h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.37,3.08&amp;year=1778">What was here in 1778? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
